#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179 :topic/id #uuid "8633737d-b07f-5466-99f0-a422586920c4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172035 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171841 :question/id #uuid "7e264be4-288b-5105-91aa-d19fb7b8f8d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8502" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d6f4a939a2c25d1706a7d54a323af2773d30ddb08bcfcada94466bb8d1b9ef9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/be/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171842 :question/id #uuid "75eea83f-0067-5aa1-a0f3-1c611ac4e949" :question/math-academy-id "e-8503" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y = -1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x = 0,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n\n- At every point along the line $y = 0,$ the slope field is horizontal" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f3/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172037 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171843 :question/id #uuid "0d0d9819-c6b4-5996-8fa8-15de909fc7a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8504" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)?$ \n\n![](images/39/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/df/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](images/4d/4d518885f30e40ed870dba7b902139fca6ccbde81f7e468631c5199e887c4923.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3351 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :topic/title "Slope Fields for Autonomous Differential Equations"}]}
